WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.970
- [Instructor] There are a few definitions

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.130
of the term citizenship.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.160
In this video, we're
going to talk about two

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.930
of those definitions.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:11.620
One is the legal definition, the status

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.330
of having citizenship.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:17.380
A person has citizenship
as a member of a state,

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.570
which gives them rights,
responsibilities, and privileges.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.980
A citizen of the United States is entitled

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.610
to the protections granted
in the Constitution

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.600
and Bill of Rights like freedom of speech.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.280
There are two paths to citizenship.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.660
People are either born
citizens because their parents

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.470
were citizens or because
they were born on US soil

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:42.810
or they can become naturalized citizens.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.380
We'll talk more in other
videos about the process

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.000
to become a citizen but for now just know

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.100
that naturalization is the legal process

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.320
by which a foreign citizen or national

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.120
can become a US citizen.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.220
The other definition of
citizenship I wanna talk about here

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.210
is not just the legal
status of having citizenship

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.750
but the practice of active citizenship.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.100
You practice good citizenship
by being an informed

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.310
and active member of
a political community.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.190
So what does it mean to be a good citizen?

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:19.070
There are a few aspects
of good citizenship.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.570
First, a good citizen is empowered.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.860
That means that they exercise their rights

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:28.220
and believe that they can
make a difference by doing so.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.927
They don't say, "Nah, I'm
not gonna do anything.

00:01:30.927 --> 00:01:32.890
"It wouldn't make a difference anyway."

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.360
A good citizen is also community-minded.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.180
They feel a sense of responsibility
for their communities

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.360
and they wanna help them be
the best that they can be.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.410
They're also inclusive.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.750
And by that I mean that
they wanna do what's best

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.700
for everyone in their
community, not just what

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.740
would be most beneficial to themselves.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.380
They include multiple
viewpoints and stand up

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.100
for the rights of others.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.460
And lastly, a good citizen is informed.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.110
They know how the government
works, they're up to date

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.020
on the problems that
the government is facing

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.380
and the decisions that it's making.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.940
Good citizens are invaluable to democracy

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.700
because democracy works best
when citizens are knowledgeable

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.083
and active in political debate.

